# cross-model-chorus: environment variables, by name only. Never commit a filled-in copy.
#
# Either export these in your shell, or copy this file to somewhere OUTSIDE every repo
# (for example ~/.config/chorus/chorus.env, readable only by you) and point the tool at it:
#   python3 chorus.py --env-file ~/.config/chorus/chorus.env ...
#   or: export CHORUS_ENV_FILE=~/.config/chorus/chorus.env
# A loaded file never overrides a variable already set in your shell.
#
# Every value in an env file (8 characters or more) is screened out of your drafts: a draft that
# contains one is refused. Entries named *_ADDRESS, *_ID or *_IDENTIFIER are flagged instead, and
# go only with --allow-flagged after you check each line. The CHORUS_* settings below are not
# screened. Keep keys and chorus settings here, nothing else.

# --- Voices. A voice whose key is missing is skipped. ---
# OpenAI, billed per call (an API key, not a ChatGPT plan).
OPENAI_API_KEY=
# Anthropic Claude, billed per call (an API key from the Claude Console, not a Claude plan).
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
# Phala's gateway: serves the two TEE voices, Kimi and GLM.
PHALA_API_KEY=
# Google Gemini: an opt-in voice that runs only when named in --voices.
GEMINI_API_KEY=

# --- Confidential runs. ---
# The gateway upstreams you approve for each TEE voice, as voice=name[,name];voice=name.
# None are approved by default, so a confidential run refuses until you set this. To see the
# upstream names a model is served from: python3 chorus.py --list-models --voices glm
# CHORUS_CONFIDENTIAL_UPSTREAMS=glm=upstream-a,upstream-b;kimi=upstream-c

# --- Guards. ---
# Extra paths never to send, separated by ':'. Each must be absolute or start with ~/. Credential
# folders such as ~/.ssh and ~/.aws, ~/.config/chorus, and the env file itself are always on
# the list.
# CHORUS_NEVER_SEND=~/finance:~/legal
# Your organization's email domains, separated by commas: addresses on them (and on their
# subdomains) are not flagged as personal data.
# CHORUS_ORG_EMAIL_DOMAINS=example.org

# --- Use cases. ---
# Extra folders of use-case .toml files, separated by ':' (see use_cases/README.md).
# --list-use-cases reads no env file, so export this in your shell if you want them listed.
# CHORUS_USE_CASE_DIR=~/chorus-use-cases
